# MCP server death: resilience under a real model. Burns one real LLM turn.
#
# Launched with ZAG_SIM_MCP_SERVER pointing at fake-mcp-die.sh, a variant that
# answers initialize + tools/list normally (advertising get_token) but EXITS
# the instant it receives a tools/call -- closing stdin/stdout without
# replying. zag's stdio transport must observe the EOF / connection-closed
# while waiting for the response and surface it to the model as a tool error.
#
# The contract under test: the failure is handled gracefully. zag must NOT
# crash (exit code 2) and must NOT hang. The turn completes -- the model gets
# an error tool result and reports failure -- then the app exits cleanly on
# /quit. This pins the EOF / "connection closed" path under a real model.
#
# A dedicated HOME, wiped fresh: no stale cache, so the model's proxy call
# forces a live connect (initialize + tools/list succeed, tools/call dies).
#
# We can't reliably anchor on the model's failure wording (it varies), so we
# wait for the turn to finish (a small idle window inside a long deadline,
# since reasoning-model streaming never goes idle mid-turn), snapshot for the
# post-run record, then /quit. A clean wait_exit is the real assertion: a
# crash or hang would surface as a non-zero / signaled exit or a timeout.

set env ZAG_SIM_MCP_HOME=/tmp/zag-sim-mcp-death-home
set env ZAG_SIM_MCP_SERVER=fake-mcp-die.sh
spawn ./src/sim/scenarios/testdata/mcp_launch.sh
wait_text /Welcome to zag/
wait_idle 500ms
send "Use the mcp tool to call the MCP tool named get_token on the server named fake (call it as mcp with server set to fake and tool set to get_token and args set to {}). If the call fails, just tell me it failed." <Enter>
# Turn runs: connect, list, then the call wedges on EOF and errors back. Wait
# for the turn to settle (3s quiet inside a 120s deadline).
wait_idle 3s 120s
snapshot death_final
send "/quit" <Enter>
wait_exit
